What is an ArrayList?
An ArrayList in Java is a resizable array (part of the java.util package).
Unlike a standard, traditional Java array—which has a fixed, unchangeable size once you create it—an ArrayList can automatically grow and shrink in memory as you add or remove elements. Under the hood, it uses a standard array to store the items, but when it runs out of space, it automatically creates a bigger array and copies your old items over.
When to Use It
⚬	When you need a list of items, but you don't know how many items you will have ahead of time.
⚬	When you need fast access to items by their index position (like list.get(2)).
⚬	When you are frequently adding items to the end of the list.
Simple Code Example (Java)
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class TestArrayList {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // 1. Create an ArrayList of Strings
        ArrayList<String> departments = new ArrayList<>();

        // 2. Add elements (it grows automatically!)
        departments.add("Surgery");
        departments.add("Cardiology");
        departments.add("Pediatrics");

        // 3. Access an element by index
        System.out.println("First department: " + departments.get(0));
        // Output: First department: Surgery

        // 4. Check the size
        System.out.println("Total departments: " + departments.size());
        // Output: Total departments: 3

        // 5. Remove an element
        departments.remove("Cardiology");

        // 6. Loop through the updated list
        System.out.println("Remaining departments:");
        for (String dept : departments) {
            System.out.println("- " + dept);
        }
    }
}

Key Characteristics of ArrayList
⚬	Index-Based: Every item has an index starting at 0.
⚬	Objects Only: An ArrayList stores objects (like String, Integer, or custom classes like MedicalUnit). To store primitive data types like int, Java automatically converts them to wrapper objects (Integer).
⚬	Dynamic Sizing: You don't have to specify a size when you create it (e.g., new ArrayList<>() is empty by default).

